Observations
------------
1. DEER iters/window is BOUNDED at ~16 even for K=2048 — independent
   of T (chaotic dynamics + approximate twist make each window harder
   than LGSSM's 2 but it does not blow up).

2. max|par-seq| has two regimes. For K<=64 it is O(1) — DEER stops at
   its float32 rtol (1e-3) and chaos amplifies the residual to state-
   scale; the linearised Newton step is still on a different attractor
   branch than the sequential reference. For K>=128 max|par-seq| drops
   to 1-6 ms (mille-units) — DEER actually converges past chaos
   amplification within rtol.

3. ESS is quite low on Lorenz (mean 0.02-0.20). The EBIF twist is a
   poor proxy for the optimal smoothing twist on a chaotic system.
   This is a twist-quality problem, NOT a parallel-correctness problem.

4. Speedup still grows roughly with K (16.4x at K=2048). Plateau is
   set by the same outer scan as LGSSM; Lorenz hits it later because
   each window's per-particle work is ~D times larger.

Summary
=======
LGSSM:    K=2048 -> 77.75x   (was 2.70x at K=32)
Lorenz63: K=2048 -> 16.39x   (was 0.83x at K=32)

The 2.7x ceiling we observed earlier was an artefact of the K=32
default. With K matched to (T, model) the speedup climbs to two
orders of magnitude on LGSSM and 16x on Lorenz63 -- all with the
same windowed-Gumbel-STE construction.
